From 4627b43565d790ad6b470825f684f4302f47bf77 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FileZilla dark mode port <fzdark@localhost>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 13:29:59 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] interface: pick icon variants matching the system
 appearance

CTheme::LoadBitmap now looks for <name>_dark.png or <name>_light.png,
choosing the one that matches the current system appearance, and falls
back to the plain artwork when a theme ships no variant. The suffix
describes the polarity of the glyph rather than that of the theme, which
is the convention renderer.cpp already follows for the macOS tree item
buttons. A name that already carries an explicit suffix is still honoured
verbatim, so those call sites keep working unchanged.

Measuring the default theme showed 7 of its 38 plain icons are effectively
invisible on a dark background - close, upload and dropdown are 100% dark
pixels, cancel 85%, uploadadd 64%, help 62% and server 42% - and 6 more
are borderline. Those 13 get a _light variant, generated by lifting each
pixel's luminance towards white while leaving alpha untouched, so the
silhouette is byte-for-byte identical to the original and colour coding
such as the green upload arrow stays readable.

The notebook tab sort arrows also stopped hardcoding ART_SORT_UP_DARK and
ART_SORT_DOWN_DARK. Those glyphs are near black, which rendered them
invisible once the surrounding UI went dark; asking for the unsuffixed
name lets the theme provider pick the right polarity.
